.TH "TRACEE\-ENRICHMENT" "1" "2026/09" "" "Tracee Enrichment Flag Manual"
.SS NAME
tracee \f[B]\-\-enrichment\f[R] \- Configure enrichment for container
events and other enrichment options
.SS SYNOPSIS
tracee \f[B]\-\-enrichment\f[R]
[container|container.cgroupfs.path=\f[I]path\f[R]|container.cgroupfs.force|container.docker.socket=\f[I]socket_path\f[R]|container.containerd.socket=\f[I]socket_path\f[R]|container.crio.socket=\f[I]socket_path\f[R]|container.podman.socket=\f[I]socket_path\f[R]|fd\-paths|environment|executable\-hash|executable\-hash.mode=\f[I]mode\f[R]|user\-stack|decoded\-data]
[\f[B]\-\-enrichment\f[R] \&...]
.SS DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]\-\-enrichment\f[R] flag allows you to configure enrichment
options for container events and other enrichment features.
.SS Flags
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]container\f[R]: Enable container enrichment with default settings.
When enabled, Tracee will enrich container events with container
information.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]container.cgroupfs.path\f[R]=\f[I]path\f[R]: Enable container
enrichment and configure the path to the cgroupfs where container
cgroups are created.
This is used as a hint for auto\-detection.
\f[B]Note\f[R]: Using this option automatically enables container, so
you don\[cq]t need to also specify \f[CR]\-\-enrichment container\f[R].
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment container.cgroupfs.path=/sys/fs/cgroup
.EE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]container.cgroupfs.force\f[R]: Force the usage of the provided
mountpoint path, skipping auto\-detection.
\f[B]Note\f[R]: This option requires \f[CR]container.cgroupfs.path\f[R]
to be set.
It cannot be used alone.
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment container.cgroupfs.path=/sys/fs/cgroup \-\-enrichment container.cgroupfs.force
.EE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]container.docker.socket\f[R]=\f[I]socket_path\f[R]: Enable
container enrichment and configure container runtime sockets for
enrichment.
Configure the path to the Docker socket.
\f[B]Note\f[R]: Using this option automatically enables container, so
you don\[cq]t need to also specify \f[CR]\-\-enrichment container\f[R].
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment container.docker.socket=/var/run/docker.sock
.EE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]container.containerd.socket\f[R]=\f[I]socket_path\f[R]: Enable
container enrichment and configure container runtime sockets for
enrichment.
Configure the path to the Containerd socket.
\f[B]Note\f[R]: Using this option automatically enables container, so
you don\[cq]t need to also specify \f[CR]\-\-enrichment container\f[R].
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment container.containerd.socket=/var/run/containerd/containerd.sock
.EE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]container.crio.socket\f[R]=\f[I]socket_path\f[R]: Enable container
enrichment and configure container runtime sockets for enrichment.
Configure the path to the CRI\-O socket.
\f[B]Note\f[R]: Using this option automatically enables container, so
you don\[cq]t need to also specify \f[CR]\-\-enrichment container\f[R].
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment container.crio.socket=/var/run/crio/crio.sock
.EE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]container.podman.socket\f[R]=\f[I]socket_path\f[R]: Enable
container enrichment and configure container runtime sockets for
enrichment.
Configure the path to the Podman socket.
\f[B]Note\f[R]: Using this option automatically enables container, so
you don\[cq]t need to also specify \f[CR]\-\-enrichment container\f[R].
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment container.podman.socket=/var/run/podman/podman.sock
.EE
.PP
Supported container runtimes for socket configuration:
.IP \[bu] 2
CRI\-O (\f[CR]crio\f[R], \f[CR]cri\-o\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
Containerd (\f[CR]containerd\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
Docker (\f[CR]docker\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
Podman (\f[CR]podman\f[R])
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]fd\-paths\f[R]: Capture the selected file descriptor\[cq]s path at
syscall entry and display it as \f[CR]fd=path\f[R].
The path travels with the event, so delays in the userspace pipeline
don\[cq]t evict it.
Only one selected descriptor is enriched per supported syscall; this
doesn\[cq]t cover every FD argument of every syscall.
Numeric values remain available to filters and detectors before output
formatting.
.RS 2
.PP
Paths can contain up to 255 bytes.
If a descriptor is invalid, capture fails, or the path exceeds the
length or traversal limit, the argument keeps its numeric value.
Directory arguments using \f[CR]AT_FDCWD\f[R] keep that symbolic value.
.PP
A path is held in kernel memory from syscall entry until the syscall
returns, so a thread blocked in a syscall keeps one entry.
The entries are preallocated when Tracee starts: about 280 bytes each,
1024 by default, and a single entry when fd\-paths is disabled.
When all entries are in use, further arguments keep their numeric value
and count as \f[CR]storage_error\f[R].
.PP
With \f[CR]\-\-server metrics\f[R],
\f[CR]tracee_fd_path_captures_total{status=\[dq]...\[dq]}\f[R] counts
\f[CR]resolved\f[R], \f[CR]unavailable\f[R], \f[CR]read_error\f[R],
\f[CR]storage_error\f[R], and \f[CR]truncated\f[R] outcomes.
These counts cover decoded event records before filtering, not events
lost in the perf buffer.
Failed \f[CR]execveat\f[R] calls can produce both entry and exit
records.
No \f[CR]\-\-capabilities bypass=true\f[R] is needed for FD path
enrichment.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment fd\-paths
.EE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]fd\-paths.max\-entries\f[R]=\f[I]number\f[R]: Set how many syscalls
can hold a path at the same time, between 1 and 1048576 (default: 1024).
Raise it when \f[CR]storage_error\f[R] grows, for example on hosts where
many traced threads block in syscalls.
Setting this option enables fd\-paths.
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment fd\-paths.max\-entries=4096
.EE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]decoded\-data\f[R]: Enable decoded\-data.
When enabled, Tracee will decode event arguments into human\-readable
strings instead of raw machine\-readable values.
This converts numeric flags, permissions, syscall types, and other raw
values into readable format (e.g., \f[CR]O_RDONLY\f[R] instead of
\f[CR]0\f[R], \f[CR]PROT_READ\f[R] instead of \f[CR]1\f[R]).
Recommended for interactive use and readability, but may add processing
overhead that impacts performance on high\-volume event streams.
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment decoded\-data
.EE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]environment\f[R]: Enable environment.
When enabled, Tracee will include execution environment variables in
process execution events (particularly useful for \f[CR]execve\f[R]
events).
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment environment
.EE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]executable\-hash\f[R]: Enable executable\-hash with default
settings.
When enabled, Tracee will compute hash values for executed binaries.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]executable\-hash.mode\f[R]=\f[I]mode\f[R]: Enable executable\-hash
and configure the mode for executable\-hash.
\f[B]Note\f[R]: Using this option automatically enables
executable\-hash, so you don\[cq]t need to also specify
\f[CR]\-\-enrichment executable\-hash\f[R].
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment executable\-hash.mode=sha256
.EE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]user\-stack\f[R] Enable user\-stack.
Presence of the flag enables it, absence disables it.
Example:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
\-\-enrichment user\-stack
.EE
.RE
